4. Dependency Mapping

This is an important section for your recruiter because it demonstrates that migration isn't simply "move VM to EC2."

Identify:

Application
    |
    +---- DNS
    |
    +---- Load Balancer
    |
    +---- Database
    |
    +---- Shared Storage
    |
    +---- Authentication
    |
    +---- External APIs
    |
    +---- Monitoring
    |
    +---- Backup
    |
    +---- Network/Firewall

Create a dependency matrix.

Source	Destination	Port	Dependency	Criticality
App	PostgreSQL	5432	Database	Critical
App	File Storage	2049/SMB	File access	High
Users	Load Balancer	443	Application	Critical
App	External API	443	Payment integration	Critical
Servers	Monitoring	443	Monitoring	Medium
5. Utilization Analysis

Collect historical CPU and memory metrics.

Example:

             Average       Peak
CPU            45%          85%
Memory         55%          75%

Use this information for AWS sizing rather than blindly mapping:

8 vCPU VM → 8 vCPU EC2

Instead:

Current capacity
       ↓
Utilization analysis
       ↓
Performance requirements
       ↓
AWS instance selection
       ↓
Load testing
       ↓
Final sizing
6. Migration Strategy

Use different migration approaches based on workload characteristics.

Application servers

Rehost

VMware VM
   ↓
AWS EC2

Reason:

Minimal application changes
Faster migration
Lower application transformation risk
PostgreSQL

Potential approach:

PostgreSQL
    ↓
AWS-managed PostgreSQL
    ↓
Amazon RDS / Aurora PostgreSQL

Final selection depends on:

Compatibility
Extensions
Performance
HA requirements
Operational requirements
Application dependencies
File storage

Move shared file workloads to an appropriate AWS storage service based on access pattern.

For example:

On-Prem File Storage
        ↓
Amazon EFS / S3
